needed for ∪ on Finset. Well-formed.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.93}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99, claude_opus_5=yes 0.93"}
{"index": 4, "problem": "A biased coin lands heads with probability $p$ and tails with probability $q$, where $p$ and $q$ are real numbers, $p$ lies between zero and one inclusive, and the tails probability is no larger than the heads probability; suppose the remaining probability, that of landing on its side, is one sixth, and that the sum of the squares of the heads and tails probabilities is one half, which is the chance that two flips give the same result; prove that the heads probability is two thirds.", "candidate": "theorem biased_coin_heads_probability (p q : ℝ) (hp₀ : 0 ≤ p) (hp₁ : p ≤ 1) (hqp : q ≤ p) (htotal : p + q + 1 / 6 = 1) (hsame : p ^ 2 + q ^ 2 = 1 / 2) : p = 2 / 3 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem olymid_ref_base_5273 {p q : ℝ} (hp : p ∈ Set.Icc 0 1) (hq : q ≤ p) (hprob : 1 / 6 = 1 - p - q) (hflip : p ^ 2 + q ^ 2 = 1 / 2) : p = 2 / 3 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate quantifies p and q over ℝ, as required. The hypotheses hp₀ and hp₁ together encode that p lies in [0,1]. The hypothesis hqp states q ≤ p, matching that the tails probability is no larger than the heads probability. The equation htotal, p + q + 1/6 = 1, is algebraically equivalent to saying the remaining side-landing probability 1/6 equals 1 - p - q. The equation hsame exactly states p² + q² = 1/2. The conclusion p = 2/3 is the required result. The quantifier structure and all stated conditions agree with the problem.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The candidate declares p q : \ren, with 0 \\u2264 p \\u2264 1 (matching Set.Icc 0 1), q \\u2264 p (tails no larger than heads), total probability p + q + 1/6 = 1 (equivalent to 1/6 = 1 - p - q), and p^2 + q^2 = 1/2, concluding p = 2/3. This matches all hypotheses and the conclusion of the English problem and the reference exactly, with only rearrangement of the total-probability equation.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.95}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 1.00, claude_opus_5=yes 0.95"}
{"index": 6, "problem": "Consider a quadratic polynomial with real coefficients whose value at one is twenty, whose value at negative one is twenty-two, and whose value at its own constant term is four hundred; find the greatest value such a polynomial can take at ten. Prove that the answer is $2486$.", "candidate": "theorem quadratic_greatest_value_at_ten : IsGreatest {y : ℝ | ∃ a b c : ℝ, a ≠ 0 ∧ a + b + c = 20 ∧ a - b + c = 22 ∧ a * c ^ 2 + b * c + c = 400 ∧ y = a * 10 ^ 2 + b * 10 + c} 2486 := sorry", "reference": "theorem olymid_ref_base_6369 : IsGreatest {x | ∃ p : ℝ[X], p.degree = 2 ∧ (p.eval 1 = 20 ∧ p.eval (-1) = 22 ∧ p.eval (p.eval 0) = 400 ∧ x = p.eval 10)} 2486 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate represents the real quadratic polynomial as p(x) = a*x^2 + b*x + c, with a, b, c : ℝ. The hypothesis a ≠ 0 ensures it is genuinely quadratic. The equations a + b + c = 20 and a - b + c = 22 correctly encode p(1) = 20 and p(-1) = 22. Since c is the constant term, a*c^2 + b*c + c = 400 correctly encodes p(c) = 400. The equation y = a*10^2 + b*10 + c correctly encodes y = p(10). Finally, IsGreatest of the set of all such values y at 2486 states both that 2486 is attainable and that every attainable value is at most 2486, exactly expressing that the greatest possible value is 2486.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The candidate encodes a quadratic p(x)=ax^2+bx+c with a ≠ 0 (equivalent to degree exactly 2 for a real polynomial written this way). Hypotheses: p(1)=a+b+c=20 matches \"value at one is twenty\"; p(-1)=a-b+c=22 matches; the constant term is c = p(0), and p(c)=a c^2 + b c + c = 400 matches \"value at its own constant term is four hundred\". The set is {y | ∃ a b c, ... ∧ y = 100a + 10b + c}, i.e., attainable values of p(10), and IsGreatest ... 2486 asserts 2486 is in the set and is an upper bound — exactly \"the greatest value at ten is 2486\". Domains are all real,