umber m and whole-number n, with hypotheses 0 < m and 2 ≤ n. It represents the n positive real numbers as x : Fin n → ℝ and assumes ∀ i, 0 < x i. It states that their finite sum is a and separately includes 0 < a, as in the problem. The conclusion sums xᵢ^m / (a - xᵢ) over all indices and gives the stated lower bound a^(m-1) / ((n-1)·n^(m-2)). The candidate uses integer exponents on the right, which faithfully interprets m-2 even when m = 1 (giving exponent -1); under n ≥ 2, real subtraction n - 1 also agrees with ordinary whole-number subtraction. The quantifiers, domains, hypotheses, and inequality direction all match the problem.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "claude_opus_5=yes 0.85, gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99"}
