{"index": 28, "problem": "Fix a rectangular coordinate system in space, take any collection of points in space whatsoever, and form its three shadows, namely the sets of feet of perpendiculars dropped from its points onto each of the three coordinate planes; counting the points of a collection with the convention that a collection with infinitely many points is counted as having none, prove that the square of the number of points in the original collection is at most the product of the numbers of points in its three shadows.", "candidate": "theorem three_coordinate_shadows_bound (S : Set (ℝ × ℝ × ℝ)) : (Nat.card S) ^ 2 ≤ Nat.card ((fun p : ℝ × ℝ × ℝ => (p.1, p.2.1, (0 : ℝ))) '' S) * Nat.card ((fun p : ℝ × ℝ × ℝ => (p.1, (0 : ℝ), p.2.2)) '' S) * Nat.card ((fun p : ℝ × ℝ × ℝ => ((0 : ℝ), p.2.1, p.2.2)) '' S) := by sorry", "reference": "theorem theorem_proving_zh_blue_1134 (S : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 3))) : S.ncard^2 ≤ (S.image (λ p => (p 1, p 2))).ncard * (S.image (λ p => (p 0, p 2))).ncard * (S.image (λ p => (p 0, p 1))).ncard := by sorry", "verdicts": {"claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: for any set S of points in R^3, (#S)^2 \\u2264 product of sizes of the three coordinate-plane projections, with infinite counted as 0. Candidate: S : Set (\\u211d \\u00d7 \\u211d \\u00d7 \\u211d), uses Nat.card of the subtype coercion (equal to S.ncard) and the three images under maps zeroing one coordinate. Zeroing a coordinate gives a set in bijection with the pair projection, so cardinalities agree with the reference's pair projections. Nat.card of an infinite type is 0, matching the convention. Quantifier: universally over all sets S (implicit via theorem binder). Conclusion matches, with the same non-strict inequality and product of three shadow counts.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.92}, "gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate quantifies over an arbitrary set S of points in three-dimensional real coordinate space, encoded as ℝ × ℝ × ℝ. 