a p 0 = 0, p(2n) = q·p n, p(2n+1) = q·p n + 1. This recursion uniquely determines p and matches 'read the binary digits of n in base q': appending digit 0 multiplies by q, appending digit 1 multiplies by q and adds 1. Consistency at n=0 holds (p0 = q·p0 = 0), so the hypotheses are satisfiable and pin down exactly the intended function, equivalent to the reference's explicit digit-sum formula. The hypothesis hq gives golden ratio (1+√5)/2 < q < 2, with q : ℝ, as stated. The conclusion asserts the set of positive k such that no positive integer m has p(2k) < p m < p(2k+1) is infinite, which is equivalent to the reference's ∀ N, ∃ k > N form and to 'infinitely many positive integers k'. Quantifier structure and strictness of inequalities match.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.9}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99, claude_opus_5=yes 0.90"}
