{"index": 64, "problem": "In a complete quadrilateral whose six points are labelled A, B, C, D, E, F, extend the diagonal through A and D until it crosses the diagonal through C and E, and call that crossing point G; let the first number be the ratio of the distance from A to D to the distance from D to G, the second the ratio of the distance from C to D to the distance from D to F, and the third the ratio of the distance from E to D to the distance from D to B, and let the first ratio be that of the distance from E to F to the distance from F to A, the second ratio that of the distance from C to G to the distance from G to E, and the third ratio that of the distance from A to B to the distance from B to C; prove that the first ratio equals one less than the product of the first two numbers divided by one more than the first number, and also equals one more than the first number divided by one less than the product of the first and third numbers, and also equals one more than the second number divided by one more than the third number; that the second ratio equals one less than the product of the second and third numbers divided by one more than the second number, and also equals one more than the second number divided by one less than the product of the second and first numbers, and also equals one more than the third number divided by one more than the first number; and that the third ratio equals one less than the product of the third and first numbers divided by one more than the third number, and also equals one more than the third number divided by one less than the product of the third and second numbers, and also equals one more than the first number divided by one more than the second number.", "candidate": "theorem complete_quadrilateral_ratio_identities (A B C D E F G : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) (x y z : ℝ) (hABC : Collinear ℝ {A, B, C}) (hCDF : Collinear ℝ {C, D, F}) (hEFA : Collinear ℝ {E, F, A}) (hEDB : Collinear ℝ {E, D, B}) (hADG : Collinear ℝ {A, D, G}) (hCEG : Collinear ℝ {C, E, G}) (hDG : D ≠ G) (hDF : D ≠ F) (hDB : D ≠ B) (hFA : F ≠ A) (hGE : G ≠ E) (hBC : B ≠ C) (hx : x = dist A D / dist D G) (hy : y = dist C D / dist D F) (hz : z = dist E D / dist D B) : dist E F / dist F A = (x * y - 1) / (x + 1) ∧ dist E F / dist F A = (x + 1) / (x * z - 1) ∧ dist E F / dist F A = (y + 1) / (z + 1) ∧ dist C G / dist G E = (y * z - 1) / (y + 1) ∧ dist C G / dist G E = (y + 1) / (y * x - 1) ∧ dist C G / dist G E = (z + 1) / (x + 1) ∧ dist A B / dist B C = (z * x - 1) / (z + 1) ∧ dist A B / dist B C = (z + 1) / (z * y - 1) ∧ dist A B / dist B C = (x + 1) / (y + 1) := by sorry", "reference": "theorem theorem_proving_zh_blue_1860 (A B C D E F G : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) (p1 p2 p3 lambda1 lambda2 lambda3 : ℝ) (h₀ : p1 = dist A D / dist D G) (h₁ : p2 = dist C D / dist D F) (h₂ : p3 = dist E D / dist D B) (h₃ : lambda3 = dist A B / dist B C) (h₄ : lambda2 = dist C G / dist G E) (h₅ : lambda1 = dist E F / dist F A) : lambda1 = (p1 * p2 - 1) / (1 + p1) ∧ lambda1 = (1 + p1) / (p1 * p3 - 1) ∧ lambda1 = (1 + p2) / (1 + p3) ∧ lambda2 = (p2 * p3 - 1) / (1 + p2) ∧ lambda2 = (1 + p2) / (p2 * p1 - 1) ∧ lambda2 = (1 + p3) / (1 + p1) ∧ lambda3 = (p3 * p1 - 1) / (1 + p3) ∧ lambda3 = (1 + p3) / (p3 * p2 - 1) ∧ lambda3 = (1 + p1) / (1 + p2) := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate uses seven points in the Euclidean plane and three real numbers, matching the geometric objects and the three initial ratios. The collinearity hypotheses encode the four sides of the complete quadrilateral (A-B-C, C-D-F, E-F-A, E-D-B) and the two diagonals meeting at G (A-D-G and C-E-G). The stated inequalities ensure that every distance used as a denominator in one of the six geometric ratios is nonzero; these are implicit in defining those ratios. The hypotheses hx, hy, and hz define x, y, and z as AD/DG, CD/DF, and ED/DB, respectively. The conclusion then gives all nine requested identities: three for EF/FA, three for CG/GE, and three for AB/BC. Differences such as writing x + 1 instead of 1 + x or y * x instead of x * y do not change the real-valued expressions. The conjunction structure includes every identity with the correct numerator and denominator.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.98}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem describes a complete quadrilateral with six points A..F, the diagonal intersection G = AD ∩ CE, three numbers p1=AD/DG, p2=CD/DF, p3=ED/DB, and three ratios λ1=EF/FA, λ2=CG/GE, λ3=AB/BC, then asserts nine identities.\n\nCandidate: points in EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2); x,y,z defined by hx,hy,hz exactly as AD/DG, CD/DF, ED/DB. Collinearity hypotheses {A,B,C}, {C,D,F}, {E,F,A}, {E,D,B} correspond exactly to the four lines of a complete quadrilateral (pairwise intersections give the six points A,B,C,D,E,F), and {A,D,G}, {C,E,G} encode G as the intersection of diagonals AD and CE — this matches the configuration implied by the English statement (the reference simply leaves the configuration implicit). The distinctness hypotheses (D≠G, D≠F, D≠B, F≠A, G≠E, B≠C) are nondegeneracy conditions ensuring the denominators of the ratios are nonzero, implicit in the problem.\n\nConclusion: nine conjuncts.\n- EF/FA = (xy-1)/(x+1), = (x+1)/(xz-1), = (y+1)/(z+1) — matches reference λ1 formulas.\n- CG/GE = (yz-1)/(y+1), = (y+1)/(yx-1), = (z+1)/(x+1) — matches λ2.\n- AB/BC = (zx-1)/(z+1), = (z+1)/(zy-1), = (x+1)/(y+1) — matches λ3.\nAll orderings and formulas agree with both the English text and the reference. Uses dist (unsigned lengths) like the reference.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.87}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.98, claude_opus_5=yes 0.87"}
