{"index": 200, "problem": "Given three positive angles, each less than a straight angle, that together add up to a straight angle, prove that the product of the sines of their halves is at most one eighth.", "candidate": "theorem sine_half_angle_product_le_one_eighth (α β γ : ℝ) (hα_pos : 0 < α) (hβ_pos : 0 < β) (hγ_pos : 0 < γ) (hα_lt : α < Real.pi) (hβ_lt : β < Real.pi) (hγ_lt : γ < Real.pi) (hsum : α + β + γ = Real.pi) : Real.sin (α / 2) * Real.sin (β / 2) * Real.sin (γ / 2) ≤ (1 : ℝ) / 8 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem algebra_58222 (A B C : Real) (hA : A ∈ Ioo 0 π) (hB : B ∈ Ioo 0 π) (hC : C ∈ Ioo 0 π) (hABC : A + B + C = π) : sin (A / 2) * sin (B / 2) * sin (C / 2) ≤ 1 / 8 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The candidate states three reals positive, each less than π, summing to π, and concludes sin(α/2)sin(β/2)sin(γ/2) ≤ 1/8. This matches the problem and the reference exactly (Ioo 0 π = 0 < x ∧ x < π).", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.98}, "gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The variables α, β, and γ are real numbers, appropriately representing angles measured in radians. The hypotheses state that each angle is positive and strictly less than π, matching “positive angles, each less than a straight angle.” The sum hypothesis α + β + γ = π matches their adding to a straight angle. The conclusion is exactly that sin(α/2) · sin(β/2) · sin(γ/2) ≤ 1/8 over ℝ. This agrees with the problem and the reference; the separate positivity and upper-bound hypotheses are equivalent to membership in the open interval (0, π).", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "claude_opus_5=yes 0.98, gpt_5_6_sol=yes 1.00"}
{"index": 197, "problem": "Consider the count of all positive divisors of the factorial of two thousand fourteen, and take the natural logarithm of that count; prove that the nearest integer to this logarithm is $439$.", "candidate": "theorem nearest_integer_log_divisor_count_factorial_2014 : |Real.log ((Nat.divisors (Nat.factorial 2014)).card : ℝ) - 439| < (1 / 2 : ℝ) := by sorry", "reference": "theorem omni_theorem_1743 : round (log (Nat.factorial 2014).divisors.card) = 439 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate uses the positive integer 2014, forms `Nat.factorial 2014`, takes `Nat.divisors` and its cardinality, coerces that count to ℝ, and applies `Real.log`, correctly representing the natural logarithm of the number of positive divisors of 2014!. The conclusion `|log(count) - 439| < 1/2` says that this logarithm lies strictly within one half of 439, which is exactly the condition that 439 is its unique nearest integer. The domains, fixed value, divisor count, logarithm, and conclusion all match the problem.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: nearest integer to ln(d(2014!)) is 439. Reference uses round(log(...)) = 439. Candidate states |log(card divisors of 2014!) - 439| < 1/2, i.e., log lies in (438.5, 439.5), which is the standard 'nearest integer is 439' condition. Round in Mathlib gives [438.5, 439.5); the half-integer boundary case is unattainable here (log of an integer equal to a half-integer is impossible since e^{878.5} is irrational... at minimum, the two statements coincide except on a boundary that cannot occur). Domain: card of divisors of 2014! cast to ℝ, log is Real.log — matches. No extra or missing hypotheses; conclusion asserts the same value 439.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.86}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99, claude_opus_5=yes 0.86"}
{"index": 195, "problem": "Consider all triples of positive integers, listed in nondecreasing order, that have no common factor greater than one and whose product is the two thousand twentieth power of six; show that the number of such triples leaves a remainder of six hundred two when divided by one thousand.", "candidate": "theorem count_coprime_ordered_triples : (((((Finset.Icc 1 (6 ^ 2020 : ℕ)).product (Finset.Icc 1 (6 ^ 2020 : ℕ))).product (Finset.Icc 1 (6 ^ 2020 : ℕ))).filter (fun p => p.1.1 ≤ p.1.2 ∧ p.1.2 ≤ p.2 ∧ Nat.gcd p.1.1 (Nat.gcd p.1.2 p.2) = 1 ∧ p.1.1 * p.1.2 * p.2 = 6 ^ 2020)).card % 1000 = 602 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem omni_theorem_1308 : { (a, b, c) : ℕ × ℕ × ℕ | a ≤ b ∧ b ≤ c ∧ Nat.gcd (Nat.gcd a b) c = 1 ∧ a * b * c = 6 ^ 2020 }.ncard ≡ 602 [MOD 1000] := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate ranges each component over `Finset.Icc 1 (6 ^ 2020)`, so all entries are positive natural numbers. This upper bound does not exclude any valid triple, since each positive factor of a product equal to `6 ^ 2020` is at most `6 ^ 2020`. The inequalities `p.1.1 ≤ p.1.2` and `p.1.1.2 ≤ p.2` (as written, `p.1.2 ≤ p.2`) impose nondecreasing order. The condition `Nat.gcd p.1.1 (Nat.gcd p.1.2 p.2) = 1` says the three entries have no common factor greater than one; the nested gcd is equivalent to the other usual association. The product condition is exactly that their product equals `6 ^ 2020`. Taking the filtered finset's cardinality and asserting `% 1000 = 602` exactly states that the number of such triples has remainder 602 modulo 1000.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem asks for the count of triples (a,b,c) of positive integers with a\b,b,c) ordered nondecreasingly, gcd(a,b,c)=1, and abc = 6^2020, and to show this count \\u2261 602 mod 1000. The candidate builds the Finset ((Icc 1 6^2020 \\u00d7 Icc 1 6^2020) \\u00d7 Icc 1 6^2020) and filters on p.1.1 \\u2264 p.1.2 \\u2264 p.2 (correct nondecreasing order for the nested product encoding), gcd a (gcd b c) = 1 (associativity/commutativity of gcd makes this the same as the reference's gcd (gcd a b) c = 1), and a*b*c = 6^2020. The bounds 1..6^2020 are harmless since any divisor triple lies in this range, and positivity is implied. Conclusion: card % 1000 = 602, matching the reference's Nat.ModEq statement (since 602 < 1000, x % 1000 = 602 \\u2194 x \\u2261 602 [MOD 1000]).", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.92}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99, claude_opus_5=yes 0.92"}
{"index": 201, "problem": "Suppose $x$, $y$ and $z$ are natural numbers, each greater than one, and that the $y$-th power of the number one greater than $x$ exceeds the $z$-th power of $x$ by exactly one; prove that $x$ must be two, $y$ must be two, and $z$ must be three.", "candidate": "theorem consecutive_powers_unique (x y z : ℕ) (hx : 1 < x) (hy : 1 < y) (hz : 1 < z) (h : (x + 1) ^ y = x ^ z + 1) : x = 2 ∧ y = 2 ∧ z = 3 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem algebra_478922 (x y z : ℕ) (hx : x > 1) (hy : y > 1) (hz : z > 1) (h : (x + 1)^y - x^z = 1) : x = 2 ∧ y = 2 ∧ z = 3 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate quantifies x, y, and z over ℕ, matching the problem's natural-number domain. The hypotheses hx, hy, and hz state 1 < x, 1 < y, and 1 < z, exactly expressing that each number is greater than one. The equation (x + 1) ^ y = x ^ z + 1 says that the y-th power of x + 1 exceeds the z-th power of x by exactly one. The conclusion x = 2 ∧ y = 2 ∧ z = 3 exactly matches the required result. The quantifier structure and all assumptions are preserved.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: x,y,z natural numbers each >1, (x+1)^y = x^z + 1, conclude x=2,y=2,z=3. The candidate states exactly this with hypotheses 1<x, 1<y, 1<z and equation (x+1)^y = x^z + 1, concluding the conjunction. This matches; indeed it avoids the truncated-subtraction pitfall in the reference, but is mathematically the intended statement.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.95}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 1.00, claude_opus_5=yes 0.95"}
{"index": 199, "problem": "Two sequences of real numbers are built so that the first term of the first sequence is one and the first term of the second sequence is two, and at every index the next term of the first sequence is obtained by taking one plus the current term of the first sequence plus the product of the two current terms and dividing it by the current term of the second sequence, while the next term of the second sequence is obtained by taking one plus the current term of the second sequence plus the product of the two current terms and dividing it by the current term of the first sequence; prove that the two-thousand-eighth term of the first sequence is less than five.", "candidate": "theorem first_sequence_term_2008_lt_five (a b : ℕ → ℝ) (ha₁ : a 1 = 1) (hb₁ : b 1 = 2) (ha_rec : ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → a (n + 1) = (1 + a n + a n * b n) / b n) (hb_rec : ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → b (n + 1) = (1 + b n + a n * b n) / a n) : a 2008 < 5 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem algebra_379384 (a b : ℕ → ℝ) (ha1 : a 1 = 1) (hb1 : b 1 = 2) (han : ∀ n, a (n + 1) = (1 + a n + a n * b n) / b n) (hbn : ∀ n, b (n + 1) = (1 + b n + a n * b n) / a n) : a 2008 < 5 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate uses two sequences `a b : ℕ → ℝ`, so their terms are real numbers. It sets the first indexed terms correctly with `a 1 = 1` and `b 1 = 2`. For every natural index `n ≥ 1`, it gives exactly the stated recurrences: `a (n+1) = (1 + a n + a n * b n) / b n` and `b (n+1) = (1 + b n + a n * b n) / a n`. Restricting these recurrences to `n ≥ 1` appropriately models sequences whose first term has index 1; the unused values at index 0 are irrelevant. The conclusion `a 2008 < 5` exactly asserts that the 2008th term of the first sequence is less than five.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem defines two sequences with a\ba1=1, b1=2 and recurrences for the next terms at every index, concluding a_2008 < 5. The candidate uses a b : \ninat \nto \nreal, sets a 1 = 1, b 1 = 2, and imposes the recurrences for all n \nge 1, which matches the intended indexing starting at 1 (the reference imposes them for all n, including n = 0, which is an extra constraint on unused indices a 0, b 0). Since the recurrences from n = 1 onward determine a 2008 uniquely, the candidate's statement carries the same mathematical content: prove a 2008 < 5. Formulas match exactly: (1 + a n + a n * b n)/b n and (1 + b n + a n * b n)/a n. Conclusion a 2008 < 5 matches. Types are real, indices natural. Well-formed Lean.", "differences": ["Candidate restricts the recurrence hypotheses to n \nge 1, whereas reference states them for all n (including n = 0); this does not affect the determined value of a 2008 and arguably matches the English better."], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.87}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99, claude_opus_5=yes 0.87"}
{"index": 205, "problem": "Suppose John's age and Mary's age are positive whole numbers such that John's age equals ten times the remainder of Mary's age upon division by ten, plus the whole-number quotient of Mary's age by ten, and such that the square of Mary's age subtracted from the square of John's age, understood as zero whenever that subtraction would go below zero, is a perfect square; prove that John is sixty-five and Mary is fifty-six.", "candidate": "theorem john_and_mary_ages (john mary : ℕ) (hJohnPos : 0 < john) (hMaryPos : 0 < mary) (hAgeRelation : john = 10 * (mary % 10) + mary / 10) (hPerfectSquare : ∃ k : ℕ, john ^ 2 - mary ^ 2 = k ^ 2) : john = 65 ∧ mary = 56 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem olymid_ref_base_995 {x y : ℕ} (hx : x > 0) (hy : y > 0) (hxy : x = 10 * (y % 10) + (y / 10)) (hdiff : ∃ n, n^2 = x^2 - y^2) : x = 65 ∧ y = 56 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate quantifies John's and Mary's ages as natural numbers and explicitly assumes both are positive, matching positive whole-number ages. The age relation `john = 10 * (mary % 10) + mary / 10` exactly expresses ten times Mary's remainder modulo ten plus her natural-number quotient upon division by ten. Since the ages are natural numbers, `john ^ 2 - mary ^ 2` uses truncated natural subtraction, so it is zero when ordinary subtraction would be negative, as specified. The hypothesis `∃ k : ℕ, john ^ 2 - mary ^ 2 = k ^ 2` says this truncated difference is a perfect square. Finally, the conclusion `john = 65 ∧ mary = 56` matches the requested result.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: John's age j, Mary's age m positive naturals; j = 10*(m mod 10) + (m div 10); j^2 - m^2 with truncated subtraction (zero if negative) is a perfect square; conclude j=65, m=56. The candidate uses ℕ for both, positivity hypotheses, the same age relation with % and /, existence of k with john^2 - mary^2 = k^2 (ℕ truncated subtraction matching \"understood as zero whenever below zero\"), and the conclusion john = 65 ∧ mary = 56. This matches the reference exactly up to variable names and equation orientation.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.95}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 1.00, claude_opus_5=yes 0.95"}
{"index": 202, "problem": "Suppose a sequence of rational numbers, indexed by the positive integers, starts with the value two, and each later term equals four times the exponent of the largest power of three dividing its index, plus two, minus twice the reciprocal of the preceding term; prove that every positive rational number occurs as exactly one term of this sequence.", "candidate": "theorem positive_rationals_occur_exactly_once (a : ℕ → ℚ) (h₁ : a 1 = 2) (hrec : ∀ n : ℕ, 2 ≤ n → a n = 4 * (padicValNat 3 n : ℚ) + 2 - 2 / a (n - 1)) : ∀ q : ℚ, 0 < q → ∃! n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧ a n = q := by sorry", "reference": "theorem olymid_ref_base_2712 {u : ℕ → ℚ} (hu1 : u 1 = 2) (hun : ∀ n ≥ 2, u n = 4 * Nat.factorization n 3 + 2 - 2 / u (n - 1)) : ∀ q > 0, ∃! n ≥ 1, u n = q := by sorry", "verdicts": {"claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: sequence a: positive integers → ℚ, a(1)=2, for n≥2, a(n) = 4*v_3(n) + 2 - 2/a(n-1); prove every positive rational appears exactly once. Candidate: a : ℕ → ℚ, a 1 = 2, ∀ n ≥ 2, a n = 4*(padicValNat 3 n) + 2 - 2/a(n-1), conclusion ∀ q : ℚ, 0 < q → ∃! n, 0 < n ∧ a n = q. padicValNat 3 n equals Nat.factorization n 3 for n ≠ 0, which is the exponent of the largest power of 3 dividing n — matches the reference's encoding. The uniqueness statement restricted to positive indices matches the reference's ∃! n ≥ 1. Values at n = 0 are unconstrained in both, and excluded from the conclusion by the positivity condition. Quantifier structure and hypotheses match.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.9}, "gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate uses a sequence `a : ℕ → ℚ`, while restricting the recurrence and occurrence claim to positive indices; the unconstrained value at index 0 is harmless and matches the intended positive-integer indexing. The initial condition `a 1 = 2` is exact. For every `n ≥ 2`, the recurrence states `a n = 4 * padicValNat 3 n + 2 - 2 / a (n - 1)`. For positive `n`, `padicValNat 3 n` is the exponent of the largest power of 3 dividing `n`, so this matches the stated recurrence. Finally, `∀ q : ℚ, 0 < q → ∃! n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧ a n = q` says that every positive rational is attained at exactly one positive natural-number index. Thus the domains, hypotheses, quantifiers, and conclusion agree with the problem.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "claude_opus_5=yes 0.90, gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99"}
{"index": 206, "problem": "Suppose two positive whole numbers are given, the first being at least one and strictly less than the second, and suppose we have as many positive real numbers as the first whole number indicates, arranged so that their sum equals their product; prove that the sum of these real numbers each raised to the power one less than the second whole number is at least the product of the two whole numbers.", "candidate": "theorem sum_powers_ge_product_of_integers (m n : ℕ) (hm : 1 ≤ m) (hmn : m < n) (x : Fin m → ℝ) (hx : ∀ i, 0 < x i) (hsum_prod : (∑ i, x i) = ∏ i, x i) : (m * n : ℝ) ≤ ∑ i, (x i) ^ (n - 1) := by sorry", "reference": "theorem algebra_57941 (k n : ℕ) (hk : 0 < k) (hn : 0 < n) (hkn : 1 ≤ k ∧ k < n) (x : Fin k → ℝ) (hx : ∀ i, 0 < x i) (hxs : ∑ i, x i = ∏ i, x i) : ∑ i, (x i) ^ (n - 1) ≥ k * n := by sorry", "verdicts": {"claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "Problem: given positive integers m, n with 1 \\u2264 m < n, and m positive reals whose sum equals their product, show \\u2211 x_i^{n-1} \\u2265 m*n. Candidate: m n : \\u2115, hm : 1 \\u2264 m, hmn : m < n, x : Fin m \\u2192 \\u211d, all positive, sum = product, conclusion (m*n : \\u211d) \\u2264 \\u2211 x_i^(n-1). Natural subtraction n-1 is safe since n \\u2265 2. Reference adds redundant 0 < k, 0 < n hypotheses implied by 1 \\u2264 k < n. Matches.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.95}, "gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The variables `m n : ℕ` represent the two whole numbers. The hypothesis `hm : 1 ≤ m` makes the first positive, and `hmn : m < n` gives the required strict ordering; together these also imply that `n` is positive. The family `x : Fin m → ℝ` consists of exactly `m` real numbers, and `hx : ∀ i, 0 < x i` makes each positive. The equation `hsum_prod : (∑ i, x i) = ∏ i, x i` states that their sum equals their product. Finally, `(m * n : ℝ) ≤ ∑ i, (x i) ^ (n - 1)` is precisely the claim that the sum of their `(n - 1)`st powers is at least the product of the two whole numbers, interpreted in `ℝ`.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "claude_opus_5=yes 0.95, gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99"}
{"index": 204, "problem": "Among all fractions with positive integer numerator and denominator that lie strictly between two thousand eight ninths of the way—that is, strictly between the ratio of two thousand eight to two thousand nine and the ratio of two thousand nine to two thousand ten—find the smallest possible numerator. Prove that the answer is $4017$.", "candidate": "theorem smallest_numerator_between_fractions : ((2008 : ℚ) / 2009 < (4017 : ℚ) / 4019 ∧ (4017 : ℚ) / 4019 < (2009 : ℚ) / 2010) ∧ ∀ n d : ℕ, 0 < n → 0 < d → (2008 : ℚ) / 2009 < (n : ℚ) / d → (n : ℚ) / d < (2009 : ℚ) / 2010 → 4017 ≤ n := by sorry", "reference": "theorem olymid_ref_base_3475 : IsLeast {p : ℕ | ∃ q : ℕ, p > 0 ∧ q > 0 ∧ (2008 / 2009 : ℝ) < (p / q : ℝ) ∧ (p / q : ℝ) < (2009 / 2010 : ℝ)} 4017 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate uses positive natural-number numerators and denominators, matching the problem's positive integers. It requires the represented rational number to lie strictly above 2008/2009 and strictly below 2009/2010. The first conjunction supplies a valid fraction with numerator 4017, namely 4017/4019, in that interval. The universally quantified second conjunction states that every positive-integer fraction in the interval has numerator at least 4017. Together, these establish both attainability and minimality of the numerator 4017, exactly as required.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem asks for the smallest numerator among positive-integer fractions strictly between 2008/2009 and 2009/2010, with answer 4017. The reference encodes this as IsLeast of the set of numerators p with some denominator q. The candidate encodes the same via two parts: (a) membership \\u2014 exhibiting the explicit witness 4017/4019 lying strictly between the two bounds (verified: 4017*2009 = 8070153 > 2008*4019 = 8070152, and 4017*2010 = 8074170 < 2009*4019 = 8074171, so the witness is valid),