{"index": 417, "problem": "Integrate the square root of the quantity one less than the square of the secant, over the interval running from minus one third of a straight angle in radians to one third of a straight angle in radians, and show that the answer is $0$.", "candidate": "theorem secant_integral : (∫ x in (-(Real.pi / 3))..(Real.pi / 3), Real.sqrt ((1 / Real.cos x) ^ 2 - 1)) = 0 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem u_math_881 : ∫ x in (-π/3)..(π/3), sqrt ((1 / cos x)^2 - 1) = 0 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate integrates over the interval from `-(Real.pi / 3)` to `Real.pi / 3`, exactly the interval from minus one third to one third of a straight angle (π radians). Its integrand is `Real.sqrt ((1 / Real.cos x) ^ 2 - 1)`, which is the square root of secant squared minus one. The conclusion asserts that this integral equals `0`. Thus the domain, bounds, integrand, and claimed value all match the problem.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem asks for the integral of sqrt(sec^2 x - 1) from -\\u03c0/3 to \\u03c0/3, asserted to equal 0. The candidate writes \\u222b x in (-(\\u03c0/3))..(\\u03c0/3), sqrt((1/cos x)^2 - 1) = 0, using Real.pi, Real.cos, Real.sqrt. This matches the reference exactly up to namespace qualification and parenthesization. Interval endpoints, integrand, and conclusion all agree.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.97}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 1.00, claude_opus_5=yes 0.97"}
