{"index": 446, "problem": "Prove that for any real angle, the sum of the square roots of the absolute values of its sine and its cosine is at least one and at most the fourth root of eight.", "candidate": "theorem sqrt_abs_sin_add_sqrt_abs_cos_bounds (x : ℝ) : 1 ≤ Real.sqrt |Real.sin x| + Real.sqrt |Real.cos x| ∧ Real.sqrt |Real.sin x| + Real.sqrt |Real.cos x| ≤ Real.sqrt (Real.sqrt 8) := by sorry", "reference": "theorem algebra_539177 (α : ℝ) : 1 ≤ sqrt (abs (sin α)) + sqrt (abs (cos α)) ∧ sqrt (abs (sin α)) + sqrt (abs (cos α)) ≤ 2 ^ ((3 : ℝ) / 4) := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The variable `x : ℝ` correctly represents an arbitrary real angle. The expression `Real.sqrt |Real.sin x| + Real.sqrt |Real.cos x|` is exactly the sum of the square roots of the absolute values of its sine and cosine. The first conjunct states that this sum is at least `1`. The second states that it is at most `Real.sqrt (Real.sqrt 8)`, which is the fourth root of 8. Thus the domains, quantification, and both bounds match the problem.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: for all real x, 1 ≤ √|sin x| + √|cos x| ≤ 8^(1/4). Candidate quantifies over x : ℝ, states conjunction with lower bound 1 and upper bound Real.sqrt (Real.sqrt 8) = 8^(1/4) = 2^(3/4), matching the reference's 2^(3/4). Types and quantifiers match.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.96}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99, claude_opus_5=yes 0.96"}
