{"index": 629, "problem": "Given a list of at least two real numbers, each between zero and one inclusive, prove that some term of the list other than the last one, multiplied by one minus the term immediately following it, is at least a quarter of the first term of the list multiplied by one minus the last term of the list.", "candidate": "theorem list_adjacent_product_bound (a : List ℝ) (hlen : 2 ≤ a.length) (hbounds : ∀ x ∈ a, 0 ≤ x ∧ x ≤ 1) : ∃ i : ℕ, i + 1 < a.length ∧ a.getD i 0 * (1 - a.getD (i + 1) 0) ≥ (1 / 4 : ℝ) * a.getD 0 0 * (1 - a.getD (a.length - 1) 0) := by sorry", "reference": "theorem algebra_56868 {n : ℕ} (hn : 2 ≤ n) (x : ℕ → ℝ) (hx : ∀ i ∈ Icc 1 n, x i ∈ Set.Icc 0 1) : ∃ i ∈ Icc 1 (n - 1), x i * (1 - x (i + 1)) ≥ (1 / 4) * x 1 * (1 - x n) := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate quantifies over a finite list `a : List ℝ`, matching a list of real numbers. The hypothesis `2 ≤ a.length` states that the list has at least two terms. The hypothesis `∀ x ∈ a, 0 ≤ x ∧ x ≤ 1` requires every list entry to lie in the inclusive interval [0,1]. The conclusion provides an index `i : ℕ` satisfying `i + 1 < a.length`, so both the term at `i` and its immediate successor are valid entries and the term at `i` is not the last one. It then asserts `a[i] * (1 - a[i+1]) ≥ (1/4) * a[0] * (1 - a[last])`, exactly matching the requested bound. Although `getD` uses a default value for invalid indices, all relevant indices are valid because of the length and index conditions.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.998}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: list of ≥2 reals in [0,1]; show ∃ index i not the last, with a_i(1-a_{i+1}) ≥ (1/4) a_first (1 - a_last). Candidate: list a, hlen 2 ≤ length, all elements in [0,1], ∃ i with i+1 < length (so i ranges over indices excluding the last), with a.getD i 0 * (1 - a.getD (i+1) 0) ≥ (1/4) * a.getD 0 0 * (1 - a.getD (length-1) 0). Defaults never trigger since indices are in rang