{"index": 634, "problem": "Find the last two digits of the number obtained by raising one thousand thirty-two to its own power. Prove that the answer is $76$.", "candidate": "theorem last_two_digits_of_1032_pow_1032 : (1032 : ℕ) ^ 1032 % 100 = 76 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem omni_theorem_1789 : (1032^1032) % 100 = 76 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate uses natural numbers, matching the intended integer 1032. It raises 1032 to the exponent 1032, correctly representing raising the number to its own power. Taking the result modulo 100 gives its last two decimal digits, and the conclusion states that this remainder is 76. There are no changed or omitted hypotheses or quantifiers; the problem is a closed numerical assertion.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem asks for last two digits of 1032^1032, i.e., 1032^1032 mod 100 = 76. Candidate states exactly that in ℕ. Reference identical.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.97}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "gpt_5_6_sol=yes 1.00, claude_opus_5=yes 0.97"}
{"index": 626, "problem": "Consider all points of the unit square whose two coordinates are each positive and at most one, and keep exactly those points for which the greatest integer not exceeding the base-two logarithm of the reciprocal of the first coordinate is even and the greatest integer not exceeding the base-five logarithm of the reciprocal of the second coordinate is also even; given that the area of the resulting region, written as a fraction of two coprime positive integers in lowest terms, has numerator and denominator summing to a certain value, find that sum. Prove that the answer is $14$.", "candidate": "theorem unit_square_log_region_area_fraction (p q : ℕ) (hp : 0 < p) (hq : 0 < q) (hcoprime : Nat.Coprime p q) (harea : MeasureTheory.volume {z : ℝ × ℝ | 0 < z.1 ∧ z.1 ≤ 1 ∧ 0 < z.2 ∧ z.2 ≤ 1 ∧ Even (Int.floor (Real.log (1 / z.1) / Real.log 2)) ∧ Even (Int.floor (Real.log (1 / z.2) / Real.log 5))} = (p : ℝ≥0∞) / (q : ℝ≥0∞)) : p + q = 14 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem aime_all_2004_I_12 {S : Set (ℝ × ℝ)} (hs : S = {(x, y) | 0 < x ∧ x ≤ 1 ∧ 0 < y ∧ y ≤ 1 ∧ Even ⌊logb 2 (1 / x)⌋ ∧ Even ⌊logb 5 (1 / y)⌋}) (m n : ℕ) (area_eq : (MeasureTheory.volume S).toReal = m / n) (mpos : 0 < m) (npos : 0 < n) (mncop : Nat.Coprime m n) : m + n = 14 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem: region in unit square with 0<x\\u22641, 0<y\\u22641, \\u230alog_2(1/x)\\u230b even, \\u230alog_5(1/y)\\u230b even; area = p/q in lowest terms, p+q=14.\n\nCandidate: p,q : \\u2115 positive, coprime; hypothesis that the Lebesgue volume of the set {z : \\u211d\\u00d7\\u211d | 0<z.1 \\u2264 1 \\u2227 0<z.2\\u22641 \\u2227 Even \\u230alog(1/z.1)/log 2\\u230b \\u2227 Even \\u230alog(1/z.2)/log 5\\u230b} equals p/q in \\u211d\\u22650\\u221e; conclusion p+q=14.\n\nlog(1/x)/log 2 = logb 2 (1/x), matching the reference exactly. Volume on \\u211d\\u00d7\\u211d is the product (area) measure. The ENNReal division p/q is well-defined and, for finite positive q, equals the real quotient, so the hypothesis matches the reference's toReal = m/n. Positivity and coprimality hypotheses match. Quantifier structure (universally quantified p,q with the area hypothesis pinning them down) matches the reference's idiom.\n\nNo added or dropped hypotheses; not vacuous since the area indeed is a rational number (5/8 \\u00b7 ... actually 4/9? whatever, it's rational), so some p,q satisfy the hypothesis.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.87}, "gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The variables p and q are natural numbers, with hypotheses that both are positive and coprime, correctly encoding a fraction in lowest terms. The set consists exactly of pairs of real coordinates satisfying 0 < x ≤ 1 and 0 < y ≤ 1. The base-two and base-five logarithms are correctly represented by log(1/x)/log(2) and log(1/y)/log(5), and applying Int.floor followed by Even captures the requirement that the greatest integers not exceeding those logarithms are even. MeasureTheory.volume gives the area of this subset of ℝ × ℝ. Equating that volume in ℝ≥0∞ to p/q is an equivalent way to state that its area is the positive reduced fraction p/q. The conclusion p + q = 14 exactly matches the requested answer.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.99}}, "status": "EQUIVALENT", "reason": "claude_opus_5=yes 0.87, gpt_5_6_sol=yes 0.99"}
{"index": 633, "problem": "Determine every function that assigns to each positive real number a positive real number and has the property that, for any two positive real numbers, the value of the function at the sum of the first number and the value of the function at the product of the two numbers, increased by the second number, equals one more than the product of the function's values at the two numbers. Prove that the answer is the function sending each positive real number to one more than itself.", "candidate": "theorem determine_positive_function (f : ℝ → ℝ) (hpos : ∀ x : ℝ, 0 < x → 0 < f x) (hfunc : ∀ x y : ℝ, 0 < x → 0 < y → f (x + f (x * y)) + y = 1 + f x * f y) : ∀ x : ℝ, 0 < x → f x = x + 1 := by sorry", "reference": "theorem olymid_ref_base_8958 {f : ℝ → ℝ} (hf : ∀ x > 0, f x > 0) (h : ∀ x y, 0 < x → 0 < y → f (x + f (x * y)) + y = f x * f y + 1) : ∀ x, 0 < x → f x = x + 1 := by sorry", "verdicts": {"gpt_5_6_sol": {"analysis": "The candidate takes a function `f : ℝ → ℝ`, while requiring positivity only for positive inputs, which faithfully represents a function from positive reals to positive reals despite leaving its values outside the positive reals unconstrained. The functional equation is quantified over all real `x` and `y` subject to `0 < x` and `0 < y`, and it exactly states `f (x + f (x * y)) + y = 1 + f x * f y`. This matches the English equation; the order of addition on the right is immaterial. The conclusion states that for every positive real `x`, `f x = x + 1`, exactly the claimed solution. There are no changed hypotheses, domains, quantifiers, or conclusions.", "differences": [], "equivalent": true, "confidence": 0.999}, "claude_opus_5": {"analysis": "The problem asks for all f: ℝ⁺→ℝ⁺ with f(x + f(xy)) + y = f(x)f(y) + 1 for all positive x,y, and to show f(x)=x+1. The candidate states f : ℝ → ℝ with positivity on positives (hpos), the functional equation for all positive x,y in the form f (x + f (x*y)) + y = 1 + f x * f y, and concludes f